Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Central obesity/metabolic syndrome, Hepatomegaly, Acanthosis nigricans, Bright liver on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-045
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, dull, heavy ache in the upper right side of the stomach, severe generalized fatigue, and acute anxiety regarding a potential liver tumor following a recent abnormal routine blood test.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted new-onset, continuous right upper quadrant discomfort and severe fatigue in a patient with a known history of severe metabolic syndrome and recent abnormal liver function tests. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was required to definitively rule out acute cholecystitis, ascending cholangitis, or rapid hepatic decompensation, and to provide clinical reassurance and appropriate diagnostic pathways prior to standard clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a constant, heavy pressure under my right ribs, I am exhausted all the time, and I am terrified my liver is failing."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 3-month history of mild generalized fatigue and a vague, dull, right upper quadrant discomfort that has acutely worsened into a constant, heavy ache over the past 48 hours. Denies any sharp, colicky pain, fever, nausea, or vomiting. Reports overwhelmingly asymptomatic presentation until recently, when routine screening liver function tests (LFTs) were noted to be abnormal by their regular GP. Patient has a profound history of central abdominal obesity, concomitant type 2 diabetes mellitus (poorly controlled), and severe dyslipidaemia. Complete absence of significant ongoing alcohol consumption or viral hepatitis risk factors.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but reports severe lethargy limiting physical exertion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute health anxiety driving the consultation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 115 Kg
General: Patient appears centrally obese, anxious, but in no acute distress. No scleral icterus or jaundice.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals marked acanthosis nigricans (dark, velvety hyperpigmentation) on the posterior neck and axillae, indicating profound underlying peripheral insulin resistance. Abdomen is obese. Deep palpation of the right upper quadrant reveals hepatomegaly with a smooth, firm, and non-tender liver edge extending 4 cm below the costal margin. Murphy's sign is definitively negative. No spider naevi, palmar erythema, or ascites to suggest advanced cirrhosis. The clinical presentation explicitly aligns with central obesity/metabolic syndrome, hepatomegaly, and acanthosis nigricans; a diffusely bright liver on ultrasound is expected pending formal imaging.
Environmental Observations: Sedentary lifestyle indicators present; multiple sugary beverage containers visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with Metformin and Atorvastatin.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) / Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease (MASLD) - Status: Stable (Symptomatic hepatomegaly causing capsular stretch)
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dull, constant, heavy nature of the pain rather than severe, sharp pain, the absolute absence of fever or vomiting, and a definitively negative Murphy's sign on examination.

Alcoholic Liver Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The explicit and complete absence of significant ongoing or historical alcohol consumption, coupled with the profound, overriding presence of severe metabolic syndrome markers (diabetes, central obesity).

Hepatocellular Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The smooth, firm, non-tender liver edge rather than a hard, irregular, craggy mass, and the absence of rapid, profound unintentional weight loss or decompensated ascites, though an ultrasound is required for definitive structural evaluation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with acute health anxiety driven by a new, dull right upper quadrant ache on a background of severe metabolic syndrome. An urgent out-of-hours home visit was necessary to physically examine the abdomen and definitively exclude an acute surgical biliary emergency (cholecystitis) or rapidly expanding malignant mass. Clinical examination confirmed smooth, non-tender hepatomegaly consistent with massive hepatic steatosis, stretching the liver capsule and causing the dull ache. The explicit absence of peritonism, fever, or jaundice allowed the clinician to safely de-escalate the emergency, preventing an unnecessary and highly costly presentation to the Emergency Department overnight. Extensive reassurance and targeted lifestyle modification pathways were safely initiated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the absence of an acute surgical emergency. Generated a non-urgent referral for a transabdominal liver ultrasound and updated fasting comprehensive metabolic panel.
Positive Behavioral Change: Commit to walking for exactly 15 minutes immediately after dinner every single night. This specific, immediate physical activity directly forces the muscles to consume the glucose from the meal, dramatically lowering the insulin spike that actively drives fat deposition into the liver, thereby halting the progression of the disease and reducing the liver swelling.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss ultrasound results and initiate a comprehensive weight loss and metabolic optimization plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the dull ache suddenly transforms into severe, sharp, agonizing pain, if a high fever or severe shivering develops, or if the eyes or skin suddenly turn yellow, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: An ultrasound scan is required to visually confirm the fat accumulation in the liver and definitively rule out other structural causes for the swelling.
Clinical Course: The liver is currently swollen with fat but not permanently scarred. With aggressive, sustained weight loss and diabetes control, the fat can be completely cleared and the liver will return to a normal size, resolving the dull ache entirely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The physical examination today showed no signs of a dangerous infection or blocked gallbladder. The dull ache is simply the liver capsule stretching due to the fat accumulation. However, if the pain becomes sudden and unbearable, medical care must be sought immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The severe insulin resistance indicated by the dark skin on the neck means the body is actively converting excess sugar directly into fat and storing it massively inside the liver cells. This has caused the liver to swell significantly, stretching its sensitive outer capsule and causing the constant, heavy ache. The only effective medical treatment is aggressive weight loss; shedding just 10% of total body weight will physically drain massive amounts of fat from the liver cells, immediately reducing the swelling and pain.
Actionable Steps: Completely eliminate all liquid calories, specifically sugary sodas and fruit juices, starting immediately. Liquid fructose is shunted directly to the liver and converted instantly into hepatic fat; stopping this specific intake provides the most rapid, direct relief to the overburdened liver cells.

